---
// Null-agent baseline SPS floors. Numbers are the current canonical
// (max composite_parity per dataset) under leaderboard-results/ and
// are enforced in CI by tests/test_baseline_floors.py (sb-lhoh).
// Keep in sync with docs/baseline-floors-log.md.
const nullAgentFloors = [
  { provider: "Random baseline", dataset: "GlobalOpinionQA", sps: "0.774", n: 100 },
  { provider: "Random baseline", dataset: "OpinionsQA", sps: "0.763", n: 684 },
  { provider: "Random baseline", dataset: "SubPOP", sps: "0.758", n: 200 },
  { provider: "Majority baseline", dataset: "OpinionsQA", sps: "0.715", n: 80 },
  { provider: "Majority baseline", dataset: "GlobalOpinionQA", sps: "0.690", n: 100 },
  { provider: "Majority baseline", dataset: "SubPOP", sps: "0.673", n: 200 },
];

const defences = [
  {
    number: 1,
    title: "Private holdout",
    detail:
      "Each holdout-enabled dataset is deterministically split into a public subset, whose per-question human distributions are published on the leaderboard, and a private subset whose distributions are withheld. The private share is 20% for large datasets (OpinionsQA, WVS, GSS), 30% for medium ones (SubPOP, GlobalOpinionQA), and 40% for small ones (Michigan, NTIA, Eurobarometer). A submitter who copies published distributions has no signal for the private subset, so their public and private SPS will diverge. A gap above 0.05 at production scale is flagged for review, with a wider threshold for runs with few private questions. The mechanism has limits. The split is an unsalted SHA-256 hash of the dataset name and question key. It is stable across runs by design, so ground-truth lookups stay deterministic, and anyone with the source can reconstruct which questions are held out. What stays private is the human distribution for those questions.",
  },
  {
    number: 2,
    title: "Tiered statistical validation",
    detail:
      "Every submission goes through three tiers of checks. Tier 1 verifies schema, bounds, and distribution validity. Tier 2 recomputes JSD, Kendall's τ, and composite parity from the per-question distributions and rejects any submission whose reported aggregates do not match its per-question data. Tier 3 runs statistical anomaly detectors that compare the submission against the distribution of legitimate runs. They flag implausibly perfect per-question JSD, distributions suspiciously close to the public human data, and deviation from peers in the same model family.",
  },
  {
    number: 3,
    title: "Adversarial regression suite",
    detail:
      "Our test harness keeps a set of fabricated submissions that must fail validation: pure answer-key copies, public-copy-with-fabricated-private attacks, constant-offset noise attacks, aggregate inflation, and claimed-model swaps. New detectors are tested against this suite before merge, and any change to existing detector thresholds must keep the suite passing. The suite is versioned with the validator and runs as a CI gate.",
  },
];
---

<section class="w-full max-w-4xl mx-auto py-12" id="anti-gaming">
  <h2
    class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-text dark:text-text-dark mb-2"
  >
    Anti-gaming defences
  </h2>
  <p class="text-muted mb-4 max-w-3xl">
    Our threat model and defences draw on recent adversarial analyses of AI
    benchmarks, in particular Wang et al. (UC Berkeley, 2026) [<a
      href="#anti-gaming-ref-1"
      class="text-primary dark:text-primary-light underline underline-offset-2 hover:no-underline"
      >1</a
    >]. They pushed eight widely used agent benchmarks to near-perfect scores
    without solving any of the tasks.
  </p>
  <p class="text-muted mb-6 max-w-3xl">
    SynthBench is a submission-artifact benchmark: participants submit a JSON
    file and we score it. Attacks that assume an evaluating agent running
    alongside the submission (sandbox escapes, <span class="font-mono">eval()</span>
    on agent input, binary-trojan wrappers, LLM-judge prompt injection) do not
    apply. The general pattern behind Wang et al.'s attacks, targeting the
    evaluator instead of the task, does apply. We have three lines of defence
    against it.
  </p>

  <ol class="relative border-l border-muted/30 ml-4 mb-10 space-y-6">
    {
      defences.map((d) => (
        <li class="pl-8">
          <div class="absolute -left-3 flex size-6 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-primary text-white text-xs font-bold dark:bg-primary-light">
            {d.number}
          </div>
          <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-text dark:text-text-dark mb-1">
            {d.title}
          </h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-muted max-w-3xl">{d.detail}</p>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ol>

  <h3
    class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-text dark:text-text-dark mb-2"
  >
    Null-agent baselines
  </h3>
  <p class="text-muted mb-4 max-w-3xl">
    Following Wang et al.'s recommendation, we also track two "null agent"
    baselines over time: a uniform-random baseline and a majority-class
    baseline. A model has to score above these floors for its result to be
    non-trivial. On a stable dataset, an upward drift in baseline SPS would
    indicate a bug in the scoring function. CI monitors for it.
  </p>

  <div class="mb-4 max-w-3xl overflow-x-auto">
    <table
      class="w-full text-sm border border-muted/20 rounded-md overflow-hidden"
    >
      <thead class="bg-surface/70 dark:bg-surface-dark/70 text-text dark:text-text-dark">
        <tr>
          <th class="text-left px-3 py-2 font-semibold">Null agent</th>
          <th class="text-left px-3 py-2 font-semibold">Dataset</th>
          <th class="text-right px-3 py-2 font-semibold">SPS floor</th>
          <th class="text-right px-3 py-2 font-semibold">n</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="text-muted">
        {
          nullAgentFloors.map((f) => (
            <tr class="border-t border-muted/20">
              <td class="px-3 py-2">{f.provider}</td>
              <td class="px-3 py-2">{f.dataset}</td>
              <td class="px-3 py-2 text-right font-mono">{f.sps}</td>
              <td class="px-3 py-2 text-right font-mono">{f.n}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="text-xs text-muted mb-4 max-w-3xl">
    Canonical floors are the maximum <span class="font-mono">composite_parity</span>
    observed per (provider, dataset) across every baseline run in
    <span class="font-mono">leaderboard-results/</span>. This matches the
    leaderboard's own display logic, so these are the numbers a new
    submission must beat to appear non-trivial. CI enforces
    <span class="font-mono">random-baseline SPS &lt; 0.80</span> and
    <span class="font-mono">majority-baseline SPS &lt; 0.85</span>. The
    Berkeley paper's target for the random baseline is 0.70. The remaining
    gap comes from SynthBench's
    <span class="font-mono">p_refuse</span> term, which rewards agreement with
    human DK/Refused rates. The git log of
    <span class="font-mono">docs/baseline-floors-log.md</span> is the
    canonical drift record.
  </p>
  <p class="text-muted mb-8 max-w-3xl">
    We err on the conservative side. For a scientific leaderboard, it is better
    to hold a legitimate superhuman submission for human review than to accept a
    fabricated one. Submitters whose runs are flagged can appeal. At validation
    time, the null hypothesis is that suspicious-looking data was fabricated.
  </p>

  <div
    class="rounded-lg border border-muted/20 bg-surface p-5 dark:bg-surface-dark"
    id="anti-gaming-ref-1"
  >
    <p class="text-xs text-muted mb-1">Reference [1]</p>
    <p class="text-sm text-text/90 dark:text-text-dark/90">
      Wang, Y., Mang, K., Cheung, T., Sen, S., &amp; Song, D. (2026).
      <em>How We Broke Top AI Agent Benchmarks</em>. UC Berkeley RDI.
      {" "}
      <a
        href="https://rdi.berkeley.edu/blog/trustworthy-benchmarks-cont/"
        class="text-primary dark:text-primary-light underline underline-offset-2 hover:no-underline"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
      >
        rdi.berkeley.edu/blog/trustworthy-benchmarks-cont<span class="sr-only">
          (opens in a new tab)</span
        >
      </a>
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
